n what happens  does erdogan willingly step aside what  happens to him then  uh you know what kind of um  you know justice and reconciliation can  there be in turkey you know so much has  happened over the past  ten years not just technically but then  on top of that there's the economic  problem as well whoever comes into power  next  inherits  huge economic problems  um you know and clearly you know if if  erdogan comes into power will he reverse  course in his economic policy i think  that's unlikely you know he's he's shown  himself so wedded to it  um  but if you know if the opposition comes  into power yes absolutely they're going  to reverse that that policy but  then  there's just a huge huge mess left there  to clear up you know central bank  reserves are  is scraping the floor  um  you've got  rising unemployment you've got a lot of  a huge brain drain you know a lot of  young people who  graduate with good degrees just don't  see any hope for getting a kind of  well-paid job in turkey so they've left  the country how are you going to turn  those people back so  you know  even if  um you know these coming elections  signal the end of the erdogan era i  think turkey's got a kind of really  difficult period coming up  hannah thanks for joining spectator tv  remember if you enjoy what we do at the  spectator you can subscribe to the  magazine and to our online content to  subscribe today go to  forward spectator.co.ukforwardslash tv  offer  the national health service turned 74  this week in recent years the health  service has received billions upon  billions of extra funding from the  taxpayer but public satisfaction with  the health service is at its lowest in  20 years what's going on i'm joined now  by our assistant editor isabel hardman  isabel thanks for joining spectator tv  so this week marks the 74th birthday of  the national health service but  celebrations seem somewhat muted this  year can you give us a sense of what's  going on in the nhs at the moment  well i think things are pretty pretty  miserable in the nhs at the moment which  is why you didn't see that much  celebrating in fact i i spent quite a  lot of uh  tuesday talking to nhs figures and uh it  was only sort of halfway through the the  calls and the meetings that we suddenly  remembered oh yes it's the birthday and  so that that's sort of that mentally  where they are that there's the backlog  um which uh  if you talk to people working within the  health service they'll say that they're  doing their very best to  tackle as quickly as they can and they  thought they were doing okay but the  latest wave of covid  is again really affecting them  and is slowing progress because of staff  absences and so on um  there's also  these efficiency savings that the nhs is  being expected to make it's supposed to  double its efficiency rate to 2.2 which  is a huge ask of the health service uh  in in any circumstances but with  inflation  there's a lot of worries  that that's going to be a real squeeze  in terms of how much everything costs  and that's something that amanda  pritchard who is the chief executive of  nhs england has already been laying down  markers um she gave a speech to the nhs  confederation last week where she said  that you know budgets can only stretch  so far and you can see the nhs saying  either it can't meet those targets which  pritchard at the moment is saying it can  um  or just trying to to push back against  any suggestion for from ministers  and obviously currently i believe steve  barkley is still the health secretary  he's very into efficiencies um that they  might try to squeeze things further  well i was going to say it's difficult  to talk about what the government thinks  when it comes to the nhs seeing as the  government is currently collapsing all  around us  but ministers previous to this current  upset in westminster and whoever forms  um  the next government going forward do you  think they're waking up to the realities  facing the nhs  uh  yes and no um  so i i think um  that the treasury  is not um and the reason i say that is  that um it had blocked uh very  forcefully any attempts to do proper  workforce planning and which would  in the long term  help the nhs to be more efficient  because it is hugely inefficient if you  have massive gaps on your rotors  if you have  so many vacancies within the health  service if you  cannot actually perform  the operations that you need to because  you just don't have the nurses you don't  have the doctors  and so on and uh  and this is this is a sort of ongoing  problem um in terms of the uh the other  aspect of reality which is i think a  point that you make um  a fair bit cake which is about the  actual sustainability of the nhs as it  is i mean politically that is really  really difficult and recently there was  a paper that tony blair published and  that argued that the current model is  not sustainable and i have to say having  uh just finished writing a history of  the nhs for its 75th birthday and i've  heard that phrase so many times i think  it starts to appear from the sort of  late 1960s onwards that the bevan and  beveridge model  no longer works in today's society and  the problem is is that the solutions to  that um there are the um sort of outlier  solutions uh of scrapping it and going  for something completely different  insurance-based model um which is not  only in america but in other countries  in europe and does work uh well for uh  people in in europe so we you know it's  always worth pointing out that it's not  uh the uk or america and those are the  only two models in the whole world we do  have a slightly bigger world than that  um  or changing um some of the structures  within the health service and this is  the the point that tony blair has been  making that he wants to  basically collapse the central control  over the health service and give and  integrated care boards which are  responsible for the commissioning and  provision of nhs services uh control  over pretty much everything um  that's just not going to work with the  dna of the nhs which you know was set up  to be a highly centralized highly  controlled system that was one of  bevan's objectives in nationalizing the  hospitals  it was one of the things that he said  himself that he would be the chief  receiver of complaints and ever since  then whether health secretaries have  legally been responsible for this or  legally  distanced from that they have always  ended up talking about the bedpans that  have been dropped in hospitals hundreds  of miles away they have always ended up  forcing out chief executives who aren't  performing whether or not that's their  legal duty i mean  there's a quote by alan johnson for  instance um about mid-staffs where  he had civil servants buzzing around him  saying oh you can't do this you can't do  that and he just turned around and said  piss off i'm dealing with this um and  that's that you know that's ultimately  what it comes down to is the public  expect ministers to answer questions on  everything when it comes to the health  service and i don't quite see how that's  going to change  the the spectator data hub is keeping  track of public satisfaction with the  nhs and at the moment there are actually  more people who say they're quite or  very dissatisfied with its performance  compared to those who are satisfied but  what should we read into those quite  headline statistics  yeah so there's lots of interesting  dynamics there one is the political  dynamic of who do the public blame and  they tend to blame the politicians who  they think are um  are underfunding not supporting the  health service uh properly there's also  the um the thing that um particularly  the later labour years the politicians  then really picked up which was the but  we're lucky dynamics so  you get people saying oh the nhs is in a  terrible state but we're lucky with our  local hospital and sort of still  bought into it at a local level which  can be um  hugely frustrating and disruptive  for anyone trying to embark on sensible  reforms because actually sometimes  people aren't lucky with their local  hospital and it would make more sense to  close you know the accident and  emergency into centralized services but  you never get a hospital closure that  the local politician or indeed the local  sort of vocal population are are going  to support and say you end up with  services remaining  even when it's not particularly  economical to do so  isabel in your upcoming book you  demonstrate how the nhs has been a  battleground for some of the fiercest  political contests of our time  as we look forward to an election  possibly in 2024 maybe sooner or indeed  a leadership election just within the  conservative party how is the nhs going  to figure  i think it will figure very highly uh  because  you are already getting headlines about  the number of people who are dying while  on  nhs waiting lists  you're seeing this really interesting  phenomenon  of people crowdfunding for private  medical treatment so um private medical  insurance has gone up and down depending  on who's in government the state of the  nhs but actually people who are paying  upfront rather than taking out insurance  for an operation that they need  but have been told they have a year to  wait for and in that time they worry  that they're going to get sicker or that  their quality of life is going to  plummet they just can't wait in pain for  that long so they're going onto these  gofundme sites and so on to try to cover  their their private health care um  themselves and  so  when i talk to people in the health  service and when i talk to switched on  politicians about this they see the nhs  as being in  it's probably its greatest existential  crisis since its foundation even greater  than um at the the turn of the century  in 2000 when uh people like alan milburn  tony blair were genuinely worried the  public support for the nhs was going to  was just going to disappear public  consent was was going to disappoint  you'd get middle classes um opting out  and the consent of the middle classes  was was really important at the time of  the foundation of the health service um  and so we're back in that place again  now you're gonna have a lot of people  getting very upset um in you know  hustings uh in election question times  and so on about their mother who has  been you know left to die by four  cancelled operations which was one of  the things the mavis skeet case that  prompted a big revolution in the health  service back in 2000  and that said  it isn't necessarily the case that the  nhs  always wins elections four parties and  that's the risk for labour is that they  could end up talking about the nhs uh  while not actually uh reassuring the  public or on anything else uh you look  at the 1992 election where the nhs  was a key part of labour's campaign  didn't win them that election so  so in terms of a political battleground  um  for actually winning an election i think  there's a there's a caveat there but it  will feature really heavily in the in  the campaign one last question for you  is about on the politics of this uh over  the past five or six years when it comes  to election time and there have been  quite a few what politicians promise  isn't reform it's more money um and this  has really increased uh the the funding  of the nhs uh to the point where it is  now well above the oecd  average it's increasingly difficult to  say that the nhs is underfunded do you  think politicians are going to have to  start offering something else or do you  think the public may still buy into this  idea that if we just give it a bit more  money and i'm thinking of the new  national insurance levy of course that  workers are paying now for the nhs  ketchup and the rest of it you know  do people still think that the answer is  more money or are they expecting to hear  something different from their  politicians  i think the way in which the public  reacts to attempts to reform social care  is quite instructive in this in that  they  expect things to continue to be free  even though social care isn't free for  them at the moment and um  uh  they get very upset at the idea that  they might have to um  expect a sort of lower level of service  even though the level of service and  social care is appalling as well so  um i  uh do not think that being honest with  the public about the health service  uh tends to benefit politicians and you  know we saw that in the 2017 snap  election with social care where theresa  may basically tried to uh yeah i  interviewed her about this for the book  and uh she said that the basic problem  was that they tried to be honest with  the electorate and um that it was not  something the electorate appreciated um  they don't like actually being told  about the the state of um the state of  things and how much uh it is either  going to cost or how much a health  service is going to have to change and  uh  for good or bad and you know you've  written about this kate about the the  problem with the sort of british  emotional attachment to the nhs  um brits don't want to see a change to  the model and um  they don't want to see their local  hospital killed even when as many  politicians have said to me that local  hospital is literally killing people  they will still present petitions saying  don't close this hospital um  and so  you know good luck to anyone who wants  to be honest um about the about the  alternative options but i suspect that  uh that they won't get thanked for it  but just  another example from history um kate  used to work for the iea institute of  economic affairs and they got very upset  uh  with enoch powell for saying that there  was no point in um in arguing against  the national health service as it was  because it was something that the public  wanted they wrote to him saying see how  you have comforted our enemy  i think you know if enoch powell had  sort of cottoned onto that then i  suspect that a lot of conservative  politicians today are probably going to  draw back and think no  isabel thanks for joining spectator tv  and finally do we really know what makes  us happy rory sutherland our wikiman  columnist writes in this week's magazine  that if we were all just a little more  spontaneous our lives would be better  for it to explain rory joins me now  rory thanks for joining me on spectator  tv so you write in this week's magazine  that people don't really know what makes  them happy can you explain  well it's something called effective  forecasting and it means that  essentially and this is probably for  evolutionary reasons  we're not very good at imagining or  predicting  the effect that certain life changes  will have on our enduring happiness and  in fact there's evidence that at the two  extremes someone who enjoys a lottery  win or someone who enjoys who is victim  of say a crippling illness or  bereavement  mostly after a surprisingly short time  obviously it varies their happiness kind  of returns to a baseline level  and so  our ability to predict for example we  possibly imagine that holidays will make  us happier than they really do  that would be one example where we  probably imagine that moving to a larger  house will have a longer-term effect on  our happiness well-being and enjoyment  that it really does in reality  and i think it's important because if  we're not very good at deciding in  advance what we'll enjoy  to some extent it suggests we should  leave some room for randomness  or experimentation  uh in what we do that too much planning  um is a problem in a world where we  don't really plan for happiness very  well  so is the problem not that a holiday  won't make you happy or potentially  moving to a new home won't make you  happy but that people are just too rigid  about the whole process that they  perhaps over plan  yeah i think i think that's true and of  course  i mean there's a fundamental problem  with tourism in that if you have let's  say  300 million chinese newly members of the  middle class who can probably get two  weeks vacation  and that means that once in their  lifetime they're going to go to london  and they're going to go to paris and  they're going to see the mona lisa and  they're going to see the acts of  parliament and you know and they all  visit the same tourist sites okay  because that's what they believe they  have to do in advance then there's a  fundamental problem with world tourism  and travel which is just over  concentration  and i would argue that a lot of the  things that are really charming and  enjoyable in life the things we really  like aren't necessarily the things we  assume will like  and i give the example in my article  that i much prefer chicago to new york  now if you'd asked me which is the most  exciting city in the united states i  would probably have answered i suppose  possibly las vegas possibly new york  possibly los angeles but i happened to  go on a business trip to chicago and  thought it was magical you know  architecturally it craps on uh most of  the other american cities for example  and those are the kind of things which  using advanced information you won't get  right you know  some of the nicest places i've ever been  are french towns that nobody's ever  heard of  and therefore using what we know  or what we think is important in advance  to actually plan out our lives might  actually be a mistake  can i ask rory when were you in chicago  because between november and april i  would say that is not a pleasant  experience it's actually very cold  i was first there in november bizarrely  but during kind of it during a kind of  indian summer all right actually  early november in chicago people were  swimming in the lake that was a very  warm november that's probably unusual  now i buy the fact that chicago is  climatically dependent  the only point i'd make i think is that  um if you if you're obsessed with  enjoying the things you've decided will  give you pleasure in advance you do pay  a price now in artificial intelligence  and funnily enough in the study of  animal foraging there's a name for this  and it's called the explore exploits  trade-off  there's a trade-off between exploiting  what you already know or think is  valuable  and exploring what you don't yet know  and it's possible that in a world where  we can plan our holidays down to  um absolutely  infinitesimally small details using the  internet  that we're all guilty of over planning  and the way we should go on holiday is  to say i'm going to fly into city a i'm  going to fly out of city b two weeks  later and  a large part of what happens in between  i'm going to leave down to happenstance  but rory that will intimidate a lot of  people especially  by the way it intimidates me  i mean i actually find myself having to  actually restrain my urge to plan  are there any tools out there that might  enable somebody to essentially plan to  be more spontaneous  well there is a famous idea called dice  man which is a whole concept where you  actually throw a dice to determine what  you do next or die technically i know  it's called um but um  it it is interesting one of the things  you could do is simply  uh you know plan no more than two days  ahead in a 10-day holiday for example  and i think what we do is we have an  advanced idea of perfection which we  attempt to recreate in planning our  holiday and actually when i look all i'm  saying is i look back on sort of 30  years holiday experience  and  some of the places that i've been most  delighted to have discovered  are places which i never would have  planned to visit in advance you know  they either came as a result of business  trips i went to i went to iceland which  is astounding i never would have paid to  go i'll be absolutely honest i had a  three-day business trip in iceland and  it's extraordinary you get on a plane  and three and a half hours later you  effectively land on the moon  um israel was another place i absolutely  loved without necessarily expecting it  and so a degree of um  there are two things which i think  bedevil us one of which is planning  according to what we know and fantasize  about not what happens to be out there  and the other one i think is just social  pressure i think a large part of holiday  making is actually you know bragging to  other people  um and i think the  the urge to  go somewhere which will sound cool to  other people  uh might be  profoundly limiting in fact in terms of  how much we enjoy things  well i'll keep that in mind when i  bookmark my next holiday rory i might  even bring some dice i i mean by the way  by the way i don't i don't necessarily  recommend this for every single holiday  you take  but i think i think having a certain  number of vacations which are much more  random and i agree it does take a  certain amount of courage you know even  in a uk holiday just setting off in a  car without knowing where you're going  to stay that night  um particularly in an age of course  where we're taught to book early to save  money  is just a difficult thing to do it's an  awkward feeling  but i suspect that the long-term rewards  are actually quite a bit better  rory thanks for joining spectator tv  always a huge pleasure thank you very  much indeed  that's it for this week remember if you  enjoy what we do here at the spectator  you can subscribe to the magazine and to  our online content join today and you'll  pay just one pound a week for unlimited  online and app access in your first year  to subscribe today go to spectator dot  co dot uk forward slash tv offer  and don't forget to subscribe to the  spectator's youtube channel just click  the subscribe button at the bottom of  this video and tap the bell icon to make  sure you never miss an episode  thanks ag